---
import { groups } from "../data/plugins.mjs";
---
<div class="pp-landing">
  <div class="pp-principles" aria-label="Suite at a glance">
    <div><strong>16</strong><span>focused components</span></div>
    <div><strong>01</strong><span>Git commit versions the suite</span></div>
    <div><strong>Your call</strong><span>install only what you need</span></div>
  </div>
  <section class="pp-section" aria-labelledby="work-your-way">
    <div class="pp-section-heading"><p class="pp-eyebrow">The collection</p><h2 id="work-your-way">Less friction.<br /><em>More intention.</em></h2><p>Not a new agent. A set of small, composable plugins for the OpenCode 2 you already use.</p></div>
    <div class="pp-group-grid">
      {groups.map((group) => (
        <article class="pp-group-card">
          <p class="pp-eyebrow">{group.eyebrow}</p>
          <h3>{group.title}</h3>
          <p>{group.description}</p>
          <ul>{group.plugins.map((plugin) => <li><a href={"/plugins/" + plugin.name + "/"}>{plugin.label}<span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a></li>)}</ul>
        </article>
      ))}
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="pp-section pp-workflows" aria-labelledby="in-practice">
    <div class="pp-section-heading"><p class="pp-eyebrow">In practice</p><h2 id="in-practice">A few good<br /><em>ways to work.</em></h2><p>Start with a workflow, then choose the pieces that serve it.</p></div>
    <div class="pp-recipe-list">
      <a href="/recipes/background-work/"><span class="pp-recipe-number">01</span><div><h3>Do useful work while the tests run</h3><p>Background jobs and services, delegated tasks, and waiting that stays inside the turn.</p></div><span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
      <a href="/recipes/review-loop/"><span class="pp-recipe-number">02</span><div><h3>Review what changed, line by line</h3><p>Comments and suggested edits on the last turns, sent back to the agent as one message.</p></div><span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
      <a href="/recipes/notebooks/"><span class="pp-recipe-number">03</span><div><h3>Stay with the notebook</h3><p>Follow marimo cells, wait for the kernel, keep the browser yours.</p></div><span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
      <a href="/recipes/cache-cost/"><span class="pp-recipe-number">04</span><div><h3>Know what the next turn costs</h3><p>Cache holds, context breakdowns, reasoning effort and subscription windows.</p></div><span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
    </div>
  </section>
  <aside class="pp-honesty">
    <div><p class="pp-eyebrow">Small pieces, clear boundaries</p><h2>Pick the tools.<br />Know the trade-offs.</h2></div>
    <div><p>Some features make paid classifier calls through a TypeSafe key. Some need Git, herdr or marimo. A few terminal components rely on OpenTUI internals that are not plugin API. Every reference page spells out its requirements.</p><a class="pp-text-link" href="/guides/requirements/">Read the requirements <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a></div>
  </aside>
</div>
